blocks.body:
A discrete probability space consists of a sample space $\Omega$ that is finite or can be listed as $\omega_1, \omega_2, \omega_3, \dots$ (countably infinite), and a function $P$ assigning to each event $A \subseteq \Omega$ a number $P(A)$, such that

1. $P(A) \ge 0$ for every event $A$;
2. $P(\Omega) = 1$;
3. if $A_1, A_2, \dots$ are events no two of which overlap (pairwise disjoint), then $P(A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots) = P(A_1) + P(A_2) + \cdots$.

In words: probability behaves like one unit of mass spread over the outcomes. Equally likely outcomes are the special case in which each outcome of a finite $\Omega$ carries mass $1/|\Omega|$.
